.TH NIJI-GCC 1 "" "libniji" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
niji-gcc, niji-g++ \- build programs that run on the libniji C library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B niji-gcc
.RI [ gcc-options ] " file" ...
.br
.B niji-g++
.RI [ g++-options ] " file" ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B niji-gcc
runs the system's
.BR gcc (1)
(\fBniji-g++\fR: \fBg++\fR(1)).
Compiling uses libniji's own C headers from
.I /usr/lib/niji/usr/include
(and its
.I x86_64\-linux\-gnu
directory), never the system's: the compiler runs with \fB\-nostdinc\fR, then (C++) the C++
library's directories, libniji's headers, the compiler's own include directory and the kernel
headers of \fBlinux-libc-dev\fR, after the program's own \fB\-I\fR and \fB\-isystem\fR directories.
A program that passes \fB\-nostdinc\fR gets no directory added; with \fB\-nostdinc++\fR the C++
library's directories are left out.
A link uses only libniji's start files and libraries from the sysroot
.I /usr/lib/niji
and sets the program interpreter to
.IR /usr/lib/niji/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 ,
so the program runs on libniji; the system's C library is not used.
.PP
\fB\-static\fR links libniji's static library; \fB\-shared\fR builds a shared object
against libniji.
\fBniji-g++\fR links the C++ library and the unwinder statically.
A dynamic program also links \fIniji-unwinder.o\fR, which offers that unwinder to the C
library, so a thread that exits or is cancelled unwinds (C++ destructors and \fBcatch\fR handlers run).
The loader's default library search is the sysroot only: a program that needs another
system library names its directory with \fB\-Wl,\-rpath,\fR\fIdir\fR.
\fB\-static\-pie\fR and \fB\-pg\fR are refused.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NIJI_REAL_CC\fR, \fBNIJI_REAL_CXX
The compilers (default \fBgcc\fR and \fBg++\fR).
.TP
.B NIJI_SYSROOT
Where the link-time files and the headers are read from (default \fI/usr/lib/niji\fR).
The program interpreter is always the installed loader.
.TP
.B NIJI_SYSTEM_HEADERS
When set and not empty, compile with the system's C headers instead of libniji's (libniji
implements the glibc 2.39 ABI they describe).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gcc (1),
.BR ld (1)
